.TH KEYD\-PROBE 1 "2026-09-19" "keyd-probe 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
keyd\-probe \- identify what an input device sends, and scaffold a keyd config
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B keyd\-probe
.I list
.RB [ \-\-all ]
.br
.B keyd\-probe
.I watch
.I DEVICE
.RB [ \-\-seconds
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-config ]
.RB [ \-\-output ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B keyd (1)
remaps keys by name, so the first problem is not what a key should do but what
it actually sends. Devices routinely misreport: a four\-key macropad ships as
numpad 1\-4, a volume knob ships as consumer\-control volume keys, and one
physical device can expose several kernel interfaces with its controls split
across them.
.PP
.B keyd\-probe
watches a single device, reports what each control emits in both evdev and keyd
spelling, and can print a starter configuration.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B list
List input devices grouped by USB id, which is the unit keyd's
.B [ids]
section matches. Multi\-interface devices are flagged, as are devices keyd
currently manages. Virtual devices are hidden unless
.B \-\-all
is given.
.TP
.BI watch " DEVICE"
Watch one device and report every control pressed. The device may be named by
USB id
.RI ( 0816:246e ),
by event node
.RI ( event30 " or " /dev/input/event30 ),
or by a case\-insensitive substring of its name.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-seconds " N"
Stop after N seconds. The default is to run until interrupted.
.TP
.B \-\-config
After the summary, print a starter
.B [ids]
and
.B [main]
stanza covering every control that was discovered.
.TP
.B \-\-output
Watch keyd's virtual devices rather than the hardware, showing the events your
configuration produces instead of the ones the device sends.
.TP
.B \-\-all
For
.IR list ,
include virtual and internal devices such as power buttons and the video bus.
.SH ANALYSIS
After a capture,
.B keyd\-probe
reports what the results mean. It calls out controls split across several
interfaces, keys that masquerade as a numpad and will therefore collide with a
real one unless the configuration is scoped by USB id, and keys for which keyd
has no name at all.
.PP
For rotary encoders it answers the question that decides the entire
configuration: whether the knob can report its button held
.I while
the shaft turns. Many encoders cannot; the button reports released the instant
rotation begins. Where it works, a held modifier such as
.B overload()
is possible; where it does not, only a latched
.B toggle()
will do. This is invisible until someone tries it.
.SH EXCLUSIVE GRABS
.B keyd
takes an exclusive grab
.RB ( EVIOCGRAB )
on every device it manages, so reading that device's raw nodes returns nothing
at all \- which looks exactly like broken hardware.
.B keyd\-probe
detects this and refuses rather than returning an empty capture. Either stop
keyd to see the raw hardware, or pass
.B \-\-output
to watch keyd's own output instead.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Controls were discovered and reported.
.TP
.B 1
No events were captured, the device could not be resolved, or permission was
denied.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /proc/bus/input/devices
Enumerated to discover devices and their interfaces.
.TP
.I /dev/input/event*
Read directly. Requires root, or membership of the
.B input
group.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR keyd (1)
.PP
https://github.com/lancefaul/keyd\-probe
.SH AUTHOR
Lance Faul
